BANK.push(
{"id":"i1","d":3,"q":"A new VM-Series should register with Panorama and get its template stack and device group on first boot. Where do those settings go?","o":["init-cfg.txt in the bootstrap package's config folder","bootstrap.xml in the bootstrap package's software folder","The authcodes file in the license folder","A Panorama template variable set before first boot"],"a":[0],"e":"init-cfg.txt holds basic settings like hostname, panorama-server, tplname, dgname, and the auth key. bootstrap.xml is a full config (also in config/), authcodes go in license/, and template variables don't reach an unregistered firewall."},
{"id":"i2","d":3,"q":"In a bootstrap package, which folder holds the authcodes file used to license the VM-Series?","o":["license","config","content","software"],"a":[0],"e":"The license folder holds authcodes. config has init-cfg.txt and optionally bootstrap.xml, content has content updates, and software has PAN-OS images."},
{"id":"i3","d":3,"q":"You change the bootstrap package and reboot a VM-Series that's already configured. The new settings don't apply. Why?","o":["Bootstrapping only runs on first boot or from a factory default state","The bootstrap package must be signed by Palo Alto Networks before it's read again","The firewall only reads bootstrap files from an ISO","Changes need a commit from Panorama before the firewall re-reads the bootstrap files"],"a":[0],"e":"A firewall only bootstraps when it boots without a config, on first launch or after a factory reset. Bootstrap media can be ISO, block storage, or cloud storage, and it doesn't need signing."},
{"id":"i4","d":3,"q":"Which components make up a CN-Series deployment in Kubernetes?","o":["CN-MGMT pods, CN-NGFW pods, and the CNI plugin, managed by Panorama","A single CN-NGFW pod per cluster managed from the web UI","VM-Series instances on each worker node with a sidecar proxy","CN-MGMT pods only, with traffic sent to Cloud NGFW"],"a":[0],"e":"CN-MGMT is the management plane, CN-NGFW is the data plane, and the CNI plugin steers pod traffic to the firewall. Panorama with the Kubernetes plugin manages it."},
{"id":"i5","d":3,"q":"What's the difference between CN-Series DaemonSet and Kubernetes Service deployment modes?","o":["DaemonSet runs a CN-NGFW on every node; Service mode lets CN-NGFW pods scale separately","DaemonSet only protects north-south traffic, while Service mode only covers east-west","DaemonSet needs no Panorama; Service mode needs Panorama","DaemonSet is meant for managed cloud clusters, while Service mode is for on-prem clusters"],"a":[0],"e":"DaemonSet places a data plane pod on each node to secure local pods. Service mode runs CN-NGFW pods as a scalable service that nodes send traffic to. Both need Panorama."},
{"id":"i6","d":3,"q":"A team wants NGFW protection in AWS VPCs with no OS patching, no instance sizing, and pay-as-you-go billing. What fits BEST?","o":["Cloud NGFW for AWS","VM-Series on EC2 with auto scaling","CN-Series on Amazon EKS","PA-Series in a colocation facility"],"a":[0],"e":"Cloud NGFW is a managed service. Palo Alto runs the infrastructure, and you manage rules through rulestacks or Panorama. VM-Series gives more control but means managing the instances."},
{"id":"i7","d":3,"q":"VM-Series sits behind an AWS Gateway Load Balancer. How is traffic carried to the firewalls?","o":["GENEVE encapsulation on UDP 6081","VXLAN encapsulation on UDP 4789","GRE tunnels between the subnets","IPSec tunnels from each VPC"],"a":[0],"e":"Gateway Load Balancer wraps traffic in GENEVE on UDP 6081 and sends it to the targets, which keeps original addresses intact. VM-Series supports this integration."},
{"id":"i8","d":3,"q":"What does AI Runtime Security protect?","o":["AI apps, models, and agents against threats like prompt injection and data leakage","Firewalls from bad content updates, using AI-based detection and automatic rollback","Endpoint devices from ransomware using local AI models","Kubernetes control planes from configuration drift and exposed API server endpoints"],"a":[0],"e":"AI Runtime Security (now under Prisma AIRS) finds AI apps in cloud environments and inspects their traffic for threats like prompt injection, sensitive data leaks, and malicious URLs in model output."},
{"id":"i9","d":3,"q":"With Software NGFW flexible credits, where do you define vCPU size and subscriptions for VM-Series?","o":["A deployment profile in the Customer Support Portal","An init-cfg.txt parameter named vm-credits","A licensing template pushed from Panorama","The VM's instance type in the cloud console"],"a":[0],"e":"Deployment profiles set vCPUs and security services and produce an auth code for the firewalls. The cloud instance type affects resources but not the license, and Panorama templates don't define credits."},
{"id":"i10","d":3,"q":"VM-Series on ESXi in virtual wire mode isn't passing traffic. The firewall config looks fine. What on the vSwitch is often the cause?","o":["Port groups need promiscuous mode and forged transmits allowed","The VM needs its VMXNET3 adapters replaced with E1000 adapters","DRS must be disabled on the cluster","The management vNIC has to share a port group with the data vNICs"],"a":[0],"e":"In vwire or Layer 2 modes, the VM-Series forwards frames with MACs that aren't its own, which vSwitches block by default. Allowing promiscuous mode and forged transmits on those port groups fixes it."},
{"id":"i11","d":3,"q":"An auto-scaled group of VM-Series scales in, and license credits aren't returned. What should be in place?","o":["License deactivation using a license API key configured on the firewalls or Panorama","A Panorama template setting that returns credits automatically when a VM is deleted","A lifecycle hook that deletes the VM's disk first","A content update that installs the licensing agent used by auto-scaled firewalls"],"a":[0],"e":"Instances must deactivate their licenses before termination, which uses the license deactivation API key. Without it, credits stay tied to dead instances."},
{"id":"i12","d":3,"q":"In which situation is PA-Series the clear choice over VM-Series?","o":["A data center edge needing very high throughput with decryption on dedicated hardware","A public cloud VPC with traffic that grows and shrinks heavily throughout each day","A Kubernetes cluster needing east-west pod inspection","A lab that needs many short-lived firewalls created and destroyed for testing changes"],"a":[0],"e":"PA-Series hardware has dedicated processing for high-throughput inspection and decryption at physical sites. VM-Series fits cloud and virtual environments, and CN-Series fits Kubernetes."},
{"id":"i13","d":3,"q":"How does a script get an API key from a firewall using the XML API?","o":["GET /api/?type=keygen with the admin username and password","POST /restapi/v11.1/keygen with a JSON body","GET /api/?type=op&cmd=<request><api-key/></request>","Generate it on the Device > API Keys page and copy it"],"a":[0],"e":"The XML API's keygen type returns a key tied to the admin account. Send it in the X-PAN-KEY header (preferred) or the key parameter. The REST API uses the same key."},
{"id":"i14","d":3,"q":"Which XML API call returns system info like PAN-OS version and serial number?","o":["type=op&cmd=<show><system><info></info></system></show>","type=config&action=get&xpath=/config/devices/system/info","type=report&reporttype=dynamic&reportname=system-info","type=export&category=system-info"],"a":[0],"e":"Operational commands use type=op with the CLI command written as XML. Config actions read the configuration, not live system state."},
{"id":"i15","d":3,"q":"A REST API call creates an address object.\n\nPOST /restapi/v11.1/Objects/Addresses?location=vsys&vsys=vsys1\nBody: {\"entry\":{\"@name\":\"web-srv\",\"ip-netmask\":\"10.1.1.10\"}}\n\nIt fails. What's missing?","o":["The name=web-srv query parameter matching the entry name","Basic auth must be used instead of the X-PAN-KEY header","The location must be 'shared' for any address object","The body must be XML instead of JSON"],"a":[0],"e":"REST API creates and updates need name in the query string matching @name in the body. Keys go in X-PAN-KEY, objects can live in vsys or shared, and REST takes JSON."},
{"id":"i16","d":3,"q":"An automation tool needs to run 'show session info' on each firewall. Which API covers that?","o":["The XML API with type=op","The REST API Device endpoint","The REST API Network endpoint","The REST API Objects endpoint"],"a":[0],"e":"The REST API covers config areas like objects, policies, network, and device settings. Operational commands go through the XML API type=op."},
{"id":"i17","d":3,"q":"A script commits through the XML API and immediately tries to verify the change. Sometimes it fails. Why?","o":["Commits run as jobs, so the script needs to poll the job ID until it finishes","The XML API only commits during the next scheduled commit window set on the device","Commits from the API require a reboot to take effect","The API applies changes to the running config directly, so the commit undoes them"],"a":[0],"e":"type=commit returns a job ID. The commit runs asynchronously, so poll with show jobs id until it's done before checking results."},
{"id":"i18","d":3,"q":"An old API key may have leaked. What invalidates keys on the firewall?","o":["Expire all API keys under the authentication settings, or set a key lifetime","Delete the admin account's SSH keys and generate new ones","Regenerate the firewall's master key and commit the change","Change the management interface IP so old keys no longer reach the firewall"],"a":[0],"e":"PAN-OS lets you set an API key lifetime and expire all existing keys. Changing the admin's password also invalidates keys tied to it. SSH keys and master keys are separate."},
{"id":"i19","d":3,"q":"In the XML API, what's the difference between action=set and action=edit?","o":["set adds or merges at the xpath; edit replaces the whole node at the xpath","set replaces the whole node at the xpath; edit merges new elements into it","set writes to the running config; edit writes to the candidate config","set works on objects only; edit works on policies only"],"a":[0],"e":"set merges new elements without removing existing ones. edit replaces everything at the xpath with your element, so missing children are deleted. Both change the candidate config."},
{"id":"i20","d":3,"q":"A SOAR platform needs to quarantine hosts by adding them to a dynamic address group. What's the lightest-weight method?","o":["Register IP tags through the XML API type=user-id","Create a new address object and commit for each host","Edit the dynamic address group's filter and commit","Upload a new external dynamic list every minute"],"a":[0],"e":"Tags registered with type=user-id update dynamic address group membership right away without a commit. Changing objects or filters needs commits, and EDLs refresh on an interval."},
{"id":"i21","d":3,"q":"A script changes a device group on Panorama through the API and commits. The firewalls don't get the change. What's missing?","o":["A commit-all push to the device group","A second commit on each managed firewall","A template stack refresh on Panorama","A Panorama reboot to load the config"],"a":[0],"e":"A Panorama commit only updates Panorama. Pushing to firewalls is a separate commit-all (Push to Devices) for the device groups and templates."},
{"id":"i22","d":3,"q":"Where do changes made through the XML or REST API go before a commit?","o":["The candidate configuration","The running configuration","A named snapshot saved automatically","The Panorama shared config"],"a":[0],"e":"API changes edit the candidate config, the same as GUI and CLI changes, and take effect after a commit."},
{"id":"i23","d":3,"q":"Firewall rules are managed with Terraform. Someone edits a rule in the web UI. What will the next terraform plan show?","o":["A diff between the real config and Terraform's state, proposing to revert it","Nothing, because Terraform only tracks resources it created and ignores edits","An error until the web UI change has been imported into the Terraform state","The web UI change merged into the Terraform code"],"a":[0],"e":"Terraform compares desired state, recorded state, and the real config. A manual change is drift, and the plan proposes changes to match the code. It doesn't rewrite your code."},
{"id":"i24","d":3,"q":"Why does running the same Ansible playbook with paloaltonetworks.panos modules twice not create duplicate objects?","o":["The modules are idempotent and only make changes when the state differs","Ansible caches the first run and skips the second one","The firewall rejects duplicate names silently","The playbook automatically deletes objects before recreating them"],"a":[0],"e":"The panos modules check current state and report 'ok' when nothing needs changing. That's what lets you run playbooks repeatedly without side effects."},
{"id":"i25","d":3,"q":"A Terraform run against a firewall completes, but the changes aren't live. What's the most likely reason?","o":["The run only changed the candidate config, and no commit ran afterward","Terraform changes need a firewall reboot before the new config applies","The provider only works through Panorama","The state file was stored locally instead of in a remote backend"],"a":[0],"e":"Infrastructure-as-code tools for PAN-OS write to the candidate config. A commit still has to happen, through a separate step or tool in the pipeline."},
{"id":"i26","d":3,"q":"Which method is typical for deploying CN-Series to a cluster?","o":["Helm charts or Kubernetes YAML manifests","An OVA imported into the cluster's hypervisor","A bootstrap ISO attached to each worker node","A Panorama push of the container images"],"a":[0],"e":"CN-Series is deployed with Kubernetes tools such as Helm charts or YAML manifests. OVAs and ISOs are VM concepts, and Panorama manages the firewalls after they register."},
{"id":"i27","d":3,"q":"How is VM-Series usually deployed repeatably in AWS or Azure?","o":["Cloud templates like CloudFormation or ARM/Bicep, combined with bootstrapping","Manual deployment from the cloud console, followed by a config import over SSH","Panorama auto-provisioning that creates instances without any cloud templates","Copying a running VM's disk to each new region"],"a":[0],"e":"Provider templates (or Terraform) create the network and instances, and bootstrapping configures the firewalls on first boot. Copying disks carries serials and keys that shouldn't be reused."},
{"id":"i28","d":3,"q":"VM-Series on KVM needs higher throughput. What helps the most?","o":["SR-IOV interfaces with DPDK packet processing","More vCPUs assigned only to the management plane","Switching the virtual NICs to e1000","Turning off DPDK to reduce CPU usage"],"a":[0],"e":"SR-IOV bypasses the host's virtual switch, and DPDK speeds packet handling on the firewall. e1000 is slow, and management plane vCPUs don't raise data plane throughput."},
{"id":"i29","d":3,"q":"A pipeline uses API keys to manage firewalls. Where should the keys live?","o":["In a secrets manager or Ansible Vault, injected at run time","In the playbook's variables file, committed to the Git repo","In the Terraform code as the default value of a variable","In a comment inside the bootstrap.xml"],"a":[0],"e":"API keys give admin-level access. Store them in a secrets manager or vault and inject them at runtime, never in source control."},
{"id":"i30","d":3,"q":"In AWS, Azure, or GCP, how can you pass basic bootstrap settings to VM-Series without a storage bucket?","o":["In the instance user data or custom data","In the instance's display name","In a DNS TXT record the firewall queries","In a Panorama template assigned to its serial"],"a":[0],"e":"Basic init-cfg settings can be passed in user data (AWS, GCP) or custom data (Azure). A bucket is only needed for full configs, content, or software."},
{"id":"i31","d":3,"q":"In what order are security rules evaluated on a Panorama-managed firewall?\n\nShared pre-rules / DG pre-rules / local rules / DG post-rules / shared post-rules / default rules","o":["Shared pre, DG pre, local, DG post, shared post, default","DG pre, shared pre, local, shared post, DG post, default","Local, shared pre, DG pre, DG post, shared post, default","Shared pre, shared post, DG pre, DG post, local, default"],"a":[0],"e":"Pre-rules go from shared down to the most specific device group, then local rules, then post-rules from the most specific device group up to shared, then the default rules."},
{"id":"i32","d":3,"q":"What's the split between templates and device groups in Panorama?","o":["Templates hold network and device settings; device groups hold policies and objects","Templates hold policies and objects; device groups hold interfaces and zones","Templates are scoped per firewall; device groups are scoped per geographic region","Templates hold objects; device groups hold the template stack"],"a":[0],"e":"Templates and template stacks cover the Network and Device tabs, like interfaces, zones, routing, and server profiles. Device groups cover the Policies and Objects tabs."},
{"id":"i33","d":3,"q":"A template stack has templates 'branch-std' (top) and 'global-base' (bottom). Both set the DNS server differently. Which wins?","o":["branch-std, because templates higher in the stack take priority","global-base, because the bottom template is treated as the base and wins","Whichever template was committed most recently on Panorama","Neither; the push fails with a conflict"],"a":[0],"e":"In a template stack, the template higher in the list wins for overlapping settings. Put specific templates above general ones."},
{"id":"i34","d":3,"q":"50 branches use the same template, but each needs its own outside IP. What's the cleanest way?","o":["Use a template variable for the IP and set the value per firewall","Build 50 templates that differ only in the outside interface IP","Leave the IP blank in the template and set it locally on each firewall","Use a device group variable for the interface IP"],"a":[0],"e":"Template variables ($outside_ip) keep one template and override values per device or stack. 50 templates is hard to maintain, and device groups don't hold interface settings."},
{"id":"i35","d":3,"q":"A local admin overrode a template value on a firewall. You want Panorama's value back. What do you do?","o":["Push with 'Force Template Values' to remove local overrides","Delete the template from the stack and recreate it","Run a local commit on the firewall so it re-syncs the template values from Panorama","Revert the firewall to its last running config"],"a":[0],"e":"Force Template Values on push replaces local overrides with Panorama's values. A local commit keeps the override, and deleting templates is overkill."},
{"id":"i36","d":3,"q":"An address object 'dns' exists in a parent and a child device group with different IPs. By default, which one does the child use?","o":["The child's own object","The parent's object","Neither; the push fails","The one created first"],"a":[0],"e":"By default, the object defined lower in the hierarchy wins. 'Ancestor objects take precedence' in Panorama settings flips that so higher-level objects win."},
{"id":"i37","d":3,"q":"A device group rule should apply to every firewall in the group except one. How do you do it?","o":["Use the rule's Target tab and 'Install on all but specified devices'","Move that firewall to a separate device group with its own copy of the rules","Add a local rule on that firewall to deny the traffic first","Disable the pushed rule in that firewall's local config after each push"],"a":[0],"e":"The Target tab can limit a rule to certain firewalls or exclude some. Moving the firewall changes everything else it inherits, and local rules are evaluated after pre-rules."},
{"id":"i38","d":3,"q":"You make changes on Panorama and click Commit to Panorama. What happens on the firewalls?","o":["Nothing until you push to devices","They get the change on their next config sync","They pull it at the next hourly check-in","They apply it at the next reboot"],"a":[0],"e":"Commit to Panorama only updates Panorama's running config. Firewalls change only when you push with Push to Devices or Commit and Push."},
{"id":"i39","d":3,"q":"What's needed for a new firewall to connect to Panorama?","o":["Its serial on Panorama, plus Panorama's IP and the registration auth key on the firewall","A template stack assigned to the firewall before it first boots and contacts Panorama","An IPSec tunnel between the firewall and Panorama for the management connection","The firewall's MAC address added on Panorama"],"a":[0],"e":"Panorama needs the firewall's serial number, and the firewall needs Panorama's IP plus the registration auth key. It connects on TCP 3978. Templates are assigned afterward."},
{"id":"i40","d":3,"q":"You're bringing a firewall with an existing local config under Panorama management without rebuilding it. What's the process?","o":["Import the device config to Panorama, then push the config bundle with force template values","Export the firewall config from the web UI and paste it into a new template by hand","Add the firewall to a device group and commit locally","Reset the firewall to factory defaults and rebuild it from scratch with templates"],"a":[0],"e":"Panorama can import a firewall's config into a template and device group, then push the config bundle to replace the local config with Panorama-managed settings."},
{"id":"i41","d":3,"q":"Which statement about Panorama HA is true?","o":["Config syncs between peers, while logs are handled by log collectors rather than HA","Both Panorama peers can push config to firewalls at the same time","Logs are synced between the peers over the Panorama HA link","The passive Panorama processes and validates commits on behalf of the active peer"],"a":[0],"e":"Panorama HA syncs configuration. Logging is handled by the collectors and collector groups. Only the active-primary peer pushes config."},
{"id":"i42","d":3,"q":"A regional admin should only manage policies for the 'EMEA' device group and its template. What do you set up?","o":["An access domain for EMEA and a device group and template admin role","A superuser account with a login banner limited to EMEA","A separate Panorama instance for just the EMEA region","A local admin on each EMEA firewall"],"a":[0],"e":"Access domains limit which device groups, templates, and firewalls an admin can manage, and the role profile sets the permissions. Separate Panoramas or local admins break central management."},
{"id":"i43","d":3,"q":"In the ACC, you see an odd spike in one app. What's the quickest way to narrow everything on the page to that app?","o":["Click it to add it as a global filter","Change the time range to the last 15 minutes","Export the widget to PDF","Open a custom report for that app"],"a":[0],"e":"Global filters apply to every widget in the ACC, so clicking the app lets you see who, where, and what threats are tied to it. You can then drill into the logs."},
{"id":"i44","d":3,"q":"A custom report on six months of traffic runs slowly. What makes it faster?","o":["Build it on the summary database instead of detailed logs","Run it on the firewall instead of on Panorama","Add more columns so the query can use an index","Schedule it as part of a report group so it runs overnight"],"a":[0],"e":"Summary databases hold pre-aggregated data, so long-range reports run much faster. Detailed logs are only needed for fields the summaries don't have."},
{"id":"i45","d":3,"q":"Several reports should go to management as one PDF every Monday. What do you configure?","o":["A report group with an email scheduler","A log forwarding profile with email destinations","A custom ACC tab shared with management","A syslog profile to the management mailbox"],"a":[0],"e":"Report groups combine reports into one PDF, and an email scheduler sends it on a schedule."},
{"id":"i46","d":3,"q":"The User Activity report shows no data for a user who was clearly online. What's the likely cause?","o":["No User-ID mapping for the user's IP, so their traffic wasn't tied to them","The report only covers users who also have threat logs in the time range","The summary database doesn't include user data","User Activity reports only run on Panorama, not on individual firewalls"],"a":[0],"e":"User Activity reports depend on usernames in the logs. If User-ID didn't map the user's IP (or User-ID isn't enabled on the zone), the traffic isn't attributed to them."},
{"id":"i47","d":3,"q":"A Panorama report is missing data from firewalls that don't forward that log type. What do you change?","o":["Set the report's data source to remote device data","Enable log redundancy in the collector group","Run the report from the ACC instead of Reports","Increase the log quota on Panorama"],"a":[0],"e":"Panorama reports can query the managed firewalls directly (remote device data) instead of Panorama's own logs. Forwarding the logs is the long-term fix."},
{"id":"i48","d":3,"q":"Security wants to see which SaaS apps are in use and which are sanctioned. Which report fits?","o":["The SaaS Application Usage report, using the 'sanctioned' tag on apps","The Threat Summary report, filtered by the SaaS URL categories","A Botnet report, filtered by cloud apps","The ACC Blocked Activity tab, filtered by SaaS app"],"a":[0],"e":"The SaaS Application Usage report breaks down SaaS use by risk and by sanctioned or unsanctioned status. Tag approved apps as sanctioned so the report reflects your policy."}
);
